title: 'kill croak text is engine-dependent: Podman and Docker disagree on the 409/404
  body'
updated: 2026-08-27T17:20:57Z
---

Measured on rootless Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41), 2026-08-27, while closing #29.

_request croaks with the response's `message` field. That field's content for
a failed kill is engine-specific, so anything matching on the croak text is
Podman-specific here:

  Podman  409  {"cause":"container state improper",
                "message":"can only kill running containers. <id> is in state
                           stopped: container state improper",
                "response":409}
  Podman  404  {"cause":"no such container",
                "message":"no container with name or ID \"x\" found:
                           no such container","response":404}

  Docker's swagger example for the same 409 is {"message":"Container <id> is
  not running"} -- a different shape (no cause/response keys) and entirely
  different prose.

NOT measured against Docker: there is no Docker on this machine.

Two things to decide, neither urgent:
  - whether the POD should warn that croak text is not a stable interface
    and callers should branch on nothing but the fact that it croaked
  - whether the transport should expose the status code on the exception so
    a caller can distinguish 404 from 409 without regex-matching prose.
    That second one is the useful half, and it touches Role::HTTP -- the
    seam under all modules -- so it wants its own measurement of what the
    exception object currently carries.

Related: #45 is the other case where an engine-specific error shape reaches
a caller.
